        {
          "id": 999569166,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "(I'm still wearing a mask when out with people and often pull it aside to show people I'm smiling when having a discussion.) :-)  \n  \n\nI believe that in the beginning, the sections were added filename alphabetical order.  \nThat's why the sections in the [wsdg](https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsdg_html/#wsluarm_modules) start with \n```\n11.1. Saving Capture Files\n11.1.1. Dumper\n...\n```\n\nSomeone experienced in using `wslua` is going to search for, and go to, the section they need.  \nBut a new user/developer that follows the [Wireshark's Lua API](https://wiki.wireshark.org/Lua#wireshark-s-lua-api) from the Wiki is going to presented with writing a dumper from the start.  \n\"Must be important - it's at the top of the list.\" /s\n  \nWould a better solution be to add a \"pre-wsluarm\" to the Wiki that explains how to use the `wsluarm`?\n\n(Original order of the wsluarm sections)  \n[Migrate the wslua ref man into wsug](https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/commit/06d1d9bfbba2f257e22f7705641c6e82aea61583)  \n```\n  <chapter id=\"wsluarm\">\n  <title>Wireshark's Lua API Reference Manual</title>\n  &WsLuaDumper;\n  &WsLuaField;\n  &WsLuaGui;\n  &WsLuaListener;\n  &WsLuaPinfo;\n  &WsLuaProto;\n  &WsLuaTree;\n  &WsLuaTvb;\n  &WsLuaUtility;\n  </chapter>\n\n```",
          "author": {
